.SH NAME
.PP
yaesm - a configurable backup system that connects backup tools into\&
pipelines\&
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.nf
\fR\&
yaesm --help\&
yaesm --version\&
yaesm [OPTIONS] SUBCOMMAND [SUBCOMMAND_OPTIONS]\&
\fR\&
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Yaesm is a configurable backup system.\&
A single configuration file defines the backups it runs and how they\&
behave.\&
.PP
Each backup is a pipeline assembled from components.\&
It reads data from a source, may compress, encrypt, or otherwise\&
transform it, and stores the result at a destination.\&
Components may run locally or through OpenSSH, enabling local backups,\&
remote transfers, and replication of existing backups.\&
.PP
Yaesm runs as a scheduler daemon, executing backups according to their\&
schedules and accepting on-demand requests through a local control\&
socket.\&
A backup may have multiple schedules, each with its own retention\&
policy.\&
Related backups can be collected into groups and operated on together.\&
Its command-line subcommands include operations for starting and\&
interacting with the daemon, checking the configuration, and finding\&
stored backups.\&
.SH CONFIGURATION
.PP
The configuration file uses YAML 1.1 and is organized around three kinds\&
of top-level entries: one or more backup definitions, optional group\&
definitions, and an optional \fBsettings\fR section.\&
Each backup definition describes a pipeline, each group definition\&
collects backups and other groups under a single name, and the\&
\fBsettings\fR section controls behavior shared across backups.\&
.SS EXAMPLE CONFIGURATION
.PP
The example below shows how the pieces of a yaesm configuration fit\&
together.\&
It deliberately includes several optional features; a basic\&
configuration can be much shorter, and each part is fully explained in\&
the following sections.\&
.IP
.nf
\fR\&
settings:\&
  ssh:\&
    identity_file: /root/.ssh/id_ed25519\&
    config_file: /root/.ssh/config\&
  scheduler:\&
    timezone: America/New_York\&
    max_concurrent_backups: 2\&

primary-backups:\&
  group:\&
    - encrypted-home\&
    - root-snapshots\&

encrypted-home:\&
  previous_names:\&
    - home-archive\&
  ssh:\&
    endpoint: ssh://server.example.com\&
  source:\&
    driver:\&
      directory: /home\&
    remote: true\&
  transforms:\&
    - driver: tar\&
      remote: true\&
    - driver:\&
        zstd: 3\&
      remote: true\&
    - driver:\&
        gpg: /etc/yaesm/backup-key.asc\&
      remote: true\&
  destination:\&
    file: /srv/backups\&
  schedules:\&
    hourly:\&
      previous_names:\&
        - frequent\&
      trigger:\&
        cron: \[dq]0 * * * *\[dq]\&
      retention:\&
        - keep-last: 24\&
        - keep-for: 7d\&
    manual:\&
      trigger: on-demand\&
      retention: keep-all\&

offsite-copy:\&
  skip_unchanged: true\&
  ssh:\&
    endpoint: ssh://backup.example.com\&
  source:\&
    backup: encrypted-home\&
  destination:\&
    driver:\&
      file: /srv/backups\&
    remote: true\&

root-snapshots:\&
  source:\&
    btrfs: /\&
  destination:\&
    btrfs: /.snapshots/yaesm\&
\fR\&
.fi
.SS BACKUP DEFINITIONS
.PP
Backup definitions are the core of yaesm\[aq]s configuration: they\&
determine what data is backed up, how it is processed, and where it is\&
stored.\&
Every top-level entry other than \fBsettings\fR and group\&
definitions is treated as a backup definition.\&
The entry\[aq]s key is the backup name, and its value is a mapping that\&
defines the backup pipeline and its behavior.\&
.PP
Backup definitions share a common structure, while the \fBsource\fR,\&
\fBtransforms\fR, and \fBdestination\fR fields select the\&
drivers that make up the pipeline.\&
This section documents the common structure; see DRIVERS for the\&
settings and supported roles of each driver.\&
.SS BACKUP NAMES
.PP
The top-level key of a backup definition is its canonical name.\&
A backup name must contain between 1 and 64 ASCII letters, digits,\&
underscores, or hyphens; its first character cannot be a hyphen.\&
The name \fBsettings\fR is reserved regardless of capitalization.\&
.PP
The optional \fBprevious_names\fR field is a list of earlier names\&
for the backup.\&
These names act as aliases, allowing existing backup artifacts and\&
configuration references to remain associated with the backup after it\&
is renamed.\&
Each previous name follows the same naming rules and must be distinct\&
from the canonical name and every other previous name.\&
.SS PIPELINE
.PP
Each backup definition requires a \fBsource\fR and a\&
\fBdestination\fR.\&
The \fBsource\fR selects the driver that supplies the data, and the\&
\fBdestination\fR selects the driver that stores the resulting\&
backup.\&
A source may instead refer to another configured backup, allowing its\&
artifacts to be copied through a new pipeline.\&
Yaesm validates each pipeline while reading the configuration and\&
rejects it if its drivers cannot exchange a compatible form of backup\&
data.\&
.PP
The optional \fBtransforms\fR field is a list of drivers that\&
process the data between the source and destination, in the order\&
listed.\&
Omitting \fBtransforms\fR defines a pipeline with no explicit\&
transformations.\&
A driver with no settings is specified by name; a configured driver is a\&
mapping from its name to its value.\&
To attach settings to the pipeline step itself, use an expanded\&
definition whose \fBdriver\fR field contains either form.\&
See DRIVERS for the available drivers and their settings.\&
.SS SSH
.PP
The optional \fBssh\fR field configures at most one OpenSSH endpoint\&
for a backup.\&
To run a source, transform, or destination there, use an expanded driver\&
definition and set its \fBremote\fR field to \fBtrue\fR; drivers\&
run locally otherwise.\&
Pipelines may therefore be local-to-local, local-to-remote,\&
remote-to-local, or remote-to-remote on the same remote system; a\&
pipeline cannot directly connect two different remote systems.\&
.PP
The \fBssh\fR field is a mapping that requires an \fBendpoint\fR\&
in the form \fBssh://[user\[at]]host[:port]\fR.\&
The \fBidentity_file\fR and \fBconfig_file\fR settings may also\&
be specified here, overriding the corresponding defaults from the\&
top-level \fBsettings\fR section.\&
An \fBidentity_file\fR must be provided either here or in the\&
defaults.\&
.PP
Yaesm supports only non-interactive OpenSSH public-key authentication;\&
password and private-key passphrase prompts are unavailable.\&
.SS SKIP UNCHANGED
.PP
The optional \fBskip_unchanged\fR field is a boolean that defaults\&
to \fBfalse\fR.\&
When enabled and an earlier artifact exists, yaesm compares the current\&
source with the newest artifact for the backup and skips the pipeline if\&
they are unchanged, regardless of which schedule created that artifact.\&
Direct pipelines require a destination driver that supports change\&
detection; pipelines that copy an existing backup compare artifact\&
identities.\&
If yaesm cannot determine whether the source changed, it reports a\&
warning and creates the backup normally.\&
.PP
For \fBfile\fR and \fBrsync\fR destinations,\&
\fBskip_unchanged\fR requires a destination filesystem that supports\&
user extended attributes (xattrs).\&
.SS SCHEDULES
.PP
The optional \fBschedules\fR field is a mapping of schedule names to\&
schedule definitions.\&
Schedule names follow the same rules as backup names.\&
A schedule may also have a \fBprevious_names\fR list, which\&
preserves its association with artifacts created under earlier names\&
after the schedule is renamed.\&
.PP
If no on-demand schedule is configured, yaesm implicitly adds an\&
on-demand schedule named \fBmanual\fR that retains all of its\&
artifacts.\&
The name \fBmanual\fR, regardless of capitalization, may only\&
identify an on-demand schedule.\&
Omitting \fBschedules\fR therefore disables timed backups but still\&
allows the backup to be run manually.\&
.SS SCHEDULE TYPES
.PP
Each schedule definition requires a \fBtrigger\fR field that selects\&
exactly one of the following schedule types.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBon-demand\fB\fR: Defines a schedule with no timer.\&
Specify it directly as the trigger name.\&
The schedule runs only when explicitly selected for a manual backup.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBcron\fB\fR: Runs the backup when a cron expression\&
matches in the scheduler time zone.\&
The expression contains exactly five whitespace-separated fields in this\&
order: minute, hour, day of month, month, and day of week.\&
Fields accept \fB*\fR, comma-separated values, ranges, and\&
\fB/\fR steps; months and weekdays may use their three-letter\&
English names.\&
All fields must match.\&
Numeric weekdays run from \fB0\fR for Monday through \fB6\fR for\&
Sunday.\&
Cron expressions should be quoted so YAML 1.1 treats them as strings.\&
.SS RETENTION
.PP
Every explicitly configured schedule requires a \fBretention\fR\&
field.\&
Its value may be one retention policy or a nonempty list of policies.\&
A policy with no settings is specified by name; a configured policy is a\&
mapping from its name to its value.\&
When multiple policies are configured, their results are combined: an\&
artifact is retained if any policy selects it.\&
Retention is applied after a backup artifact is created successfully.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBkeep-all\fB\fR: Retains every artifact created by the\&
schedule.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBkeep-last\fB\fR: Retains the specified positive number of\&
newest artifacts created by the schedule.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBkeep-for\fB\fR: Retains artifacts created within the\&
specified positive duration.\&
A duration is an integer followed by \fBm\fR, \fBh\fR,\&
\fBd\fR, \fBw\fR, or \fBy\fR for minutes, hours, days,\&
weeks, or 365-day years.\&
.SS ARTIFACT NAMES
.PP
Completed artifacts follow the base name pattern\&
\fByaesm.{backup_name}.{schedule_name}.{YYYY_MM_DD_HH:MM}.{utc_offset}\fR.\&
For example, \fByaesm.home.hourly.2026_09_01_17:00.m0400\fR was\&
created at 17:00 at UTC-04:00.\&
A period separates the backup and schedule names so hyphens within\&
either name cannot make artifact ownership ambiguous.\&
The last part of the base name is the UTC offset, with \fBp\fR for\&
plus and \fBm\fR for minus.\&
Including the offset keeps timestamps unambiguous across daylight saving\&
time transitions and scheduler time-zone changes.\&
Current and previous backup and schedule names are recognized.\&
.PP
File artifacts add format suffixes in pipeline order, such as\&
\fB.tar.zst.gpg\fR.\&
Any file or directory whose name starts with \fB.yaesm\fR is\&
internal to yaesm and should not normally be modified or removed\&
manually.\&
.SS GROUP DEFINITIONS
.PP
Group definitions collect backups and other groups under one name so\&
commands can select them together.\&
A group is a top-level mapping with a single \fBgroup\fR field\&
containing a nonempty list of backup or group names.\&
Group names follow the backup naming rules and cannot duplicate a\&
current or previous backup name.\&
.PP
A group may include other groups, and definitions may appear in any\&
order.\&
Expansion preserves member order and removes duplicate backups.\&
Previous backup names may be used as members; unknown targets and group\&
cycles are configuration errors.\&
A group cannot be used as a backup \fBsource\fR.\&
.PP
The implicit target \fB\[at]all\fR selects every configured backup\&
in definition order and cannot be redefined.\&
.SS SETTINGS
.PP
The optional \fBsettings\fR section contains system-wide\&
configuration and defaults shared by backup definitions.\&
Its entries are organized into subsections for the parts of yaesm they\&
configure.\&
.SS \fBssh\fR
.PP
The optional \fBssh\fR subsection supplies default OpenSSH client\&
settings for backup definitions.\&
A backup can override these defaults in its own \fBssh\fR\&
subsection.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBidentity_file\fB\fR: The absolute path to the private\&
identity file used for OpenSSH authentication.\&
Every backup that uses OpenSSH must specify this setting either here or\&
in its own \fBssh\fR subsection.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBconfig_file\fB\fR: The absolute path to an OpenSSH client\&
configuration file.\&
When specified, yaesm passes the file to OpenSSH with \fB-F\fR.\&
.SS \fBscheduler\fR
.PP
The optional \fBscheduler\fR subsection configures how scheduled\&
backups are run.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBtimezone\fB\fR: The time zone used to interpret backup\&
schedules and generate the timestamps in backup names.\&
The value must be an IANA time zone name.\&
The default is the system time zone.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBmax_concurrent_backups\fB\fR: The maximum number of\&
backups that may run concurrently.\&
The value must be a positive integer.\&
The default is \fB10\fR.\&
.SH DRIVERS
.PP
Drivers connect yaesm to backup tools and data formats.\&
A driver may supply source data, transform data, store a destination\&
artifact, or support more than one of these roles.\&
The following sections describe each driver\[aq]s supported roles,\&
settings, and system requirements.\&
.SS directory
.PP
The \fBdirectory\fR driver makes an existing local or remote\&
directory tree available as a backup source.\&
It does not create a snapshot or store backup artifacts.\&
.SS CONFIGURATION
.PP
The \fBdirectory\fR value is the absolute path of the directory to\&
read.\&
.SS PIPELINE ROLES
.SS SOURCE
.PP
As a source, the \fBdirectory\fR driver supplies the configured\&
directory tree to the pipeline.\&
It is a live view rather than a point-in-time snapshot, so files may\&
change while the pipeline reads them.\&
.SS file
.PP
The \fBfile\fR driver makes an existing file available as a byte\&
stream or stores a byte stream as a backup artifact.\&
It may be used locally or remotely as a source or destination.\&
.SS CONFIGURATION
.PP
The \fBfile\fR value is an absolute path.\&
As a source, it names the file to read.\&
As a destination, it names the directory beneath which artifact files\&
are stored.\&
.SS PIPELINE ROLES
.SS SOURCE
.PP
As a source, the \fBfile\fR driver reads the configured file without\&
modifying its contents.\&
It is a live view rather than a point-in-time snapshot, so the file may\&
change while the pipeline reads it.\&
.SS DESTINATION
.PP
As a destination, the \fBfile\fR driver writes the byte stream\&
produced by the pipeline to a new artifact file beneath the configured\&
directory.\&
Filename suffixes contributed by the source and transformation drivers\&
are preserved.\&
Suffixes accumulate in pipeline order, so \fBtar\fR followed by\&
\fBzstd\fR and \fBgpg\fR produces a filename ending in\&
\fB.tar.zst.gpg\fR.\&
.PP
The \fBfile\fR driver supports \fBskip_unchanged\fR when copying\&
artifacts from another backup.\&
Live file sources do not support \fBskip_unchanged\fR.\&
.SS rsync
.PP
The \fBrsync\fR driver uses the \fBrsync\fR utility to create\&
and manage directory-tree backup artifacts.\&
It may be used as a local or remote destination.\&
.SS CONFIGURATION
.PP
The \fBrsync\fR value is normally an absolute path.\&
To configure additional behavior, use a mapping containing\&
\fBlocation\fR and any of the optional settings below.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBlocation\fB\fR: The absolute path of the directory\&
beneath which backup artifacts are stored.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBexclude\fB\fR: An \fBrsync\fR exclude pattern or list\&
of exclude patterns.\&
By default, no patterns are excluded.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBone_file_system\fB\fR: Whether \fBrsync\fR should\&
avoid crossing filesystem boundaries while copying.\&
The default is \fBfalse\fR.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBextra_options\fB\fR: Additional options passed to\&
\fBrsync\fR, given as a shell-like string or list of shell-like\&
strings.\&
Each string is parsed using shell quoting rules.\&
By default, no additional options are passed.\&
.SS PIPELINE ROLES
.SS DESTINATION
.PP
As a destination, the \fBrsync\fR driver copies the directory tree\&
produced by the pipeline into a new artifact directory beneath the\&
configured directory.\&
Copies preserve filesystem metadata, symlinks, hard links, ACLs,\&
extended attributes, numeric ownership, and sparse files.\&
.PP
When an earlier compatible \fBrsync\fR artifact exists on the same\&
system, yaesm supplies it through \fB--link-dest\fR.\&
Rsync then hard-links unchanged files from that artifact instead of\&
storing their data again.\&
Every artifact remains directly browsable as a complete directory tree.\&
The \fBrsync\fR driver supports \fBskip_unchanged\fR when\&
copying artifacts from another backup: an existing copy of the same\&
source artifact is reused across schedules.\&
It does not support \fBskip_unchanged\fR for a live directory\&
source.\&
.SS btrfs
.PP
The \fBbtrfs\fR driver uses the \fBbtrfs\fR utility to back up\&
Btrfs subvolumes.\&
It can provide a subvolume as a source or store read-only Btrfs\&
snapshots as a destination.\&
.SS CONFIGURATION
.PP
The \fBbtrfs\fR value is an absolute path.\&
As a source, it names the Btrfs subvolume to back up.\&
As a destination, it names an existing directory on a Btrfs filesystem\&
beneath which snapshot artifacts are stored.\&
.SS PIPELINE ROLES
.SS SOURCE
.PP
As a source, the \fBbtrfs\fR driver takes a read-only snapshot of\&
the configured subvolume.\&
The pipeline may read that snapshot as a directory tree or export it as\&
a \fBbtrfs send\fR stream.\&
Nested subvolumes are not included.\&
.PP
A \fBfile\fR destination may store the full send stream, appending\&
\fB.btrfs\fR to the artifact filename.\&
File artifacts are always full send streams, never incremental\&
\fB-p\fR streams.\&
.SS DESTINATION
.PP
As a destination, the \fBbtrfs\fR driver stores read-only snapshot\&
artifacts beneath the configured location.\&
When the source is on the same system, yaesm first runs\&
\fBbtrfs subvolume snapshot -r\fR.\&
If the source is on another system or the snapshot command exits with a\&
failure status, yaesm falls back to piping \fBbtrfs send\fR into\&
\fBbtrfs receive\fR.\&
.PP
When matching previous snapshots exist at both ends, yaesm transfers\&
only the changes.\&
Each backup remains a complete snapshot.\&
Btrfs destinations support \fBskip_unchanged\fR.\&
.SS zfs
.PP
The \fBzfs\fR driver uses the \fBzfs\fR utility to back up ZFS\&
filesystem datasets.\&
It can provide a dataset as a source or store snapshot artifacts in a\&
destination dataset, using \fBzfs send\fR and \fBzfs receive\fR\&
when a transfer is required.\&
.SS CONFIGURATION
.PP
The \fBzfs\fR value is normally a ZFS dataset name.\&
To configure native encryption, use a mapping containing\&
\fBdataset\fR and \fBencryption\fR.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBdataset\fB\fR: As a source, the ZFS filesystem dataset to\&
back up.\&
As a destination, the dataset in which snapshot artifacts are stored.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBencryption\fB\fR: Whether to preserve native ZFS\&
encryption using raw send streams.\&
The source dataset must already be encrypted.\&
The default is \fBfalse\fR.\&
.SS PIPELINE ROLES
.SS SOURCE
.PP
As a source, the \fBzfs\fR driver takes a snapshot of the configured\&
dataset.\&
When the pipeline requires a byte stream, the snapshot is exported with\&
\fBzfs send\fR.\&
Child datasets are not included.\&
.PP
A \fBfile\fR destination may store the full send stream, appending\&
\fB.zfs\fR to the artifact filename.\&
File artifacts are always full send streams, never incremental\&
\fB-i\fR streams.\&
.SS DESTINATION
.PP
As a destination, the \fBzfs\fR driver stores snapshot artifacts in\&
the configured dataset.\&
When the source is the same dataset on the same system, yaesm creates\&
the snapshot directly.\&
Otherwise, it pipes \fBzfs send\fR into \fBzfs receive\fR.\&
.PP
When matching previous snapshots exist at both ends, the transfer uses\&
\fB-i\fR to send only the changes.\&
The resulting artifact is still a complete snapshot.\&
A \fBzfs\fR destination accepts only an unmodified ZFS send stream\&
and supports \fBskip_unchanged\fR.\&
.SS tar
.PP
The \fBtar\fR driver uses the \fBtar\fR utility to transform a\&
directory tree into an uncompressed POSIX pax archive stream.\&
It does not store the archive itself.\&
The driver works with GNU tar or bsdtar.\&
.SS CONFIGURATION
.PP
Specify \fBtar\fR by name to use its default behavior.\&
To override the default, its value is a mapping with the following\&
setting:\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBone_file_system\fB\fR: Whether \fBtar\fR should avoid\&
crossing filesystem boundaries while reading the directory tree.\&
The default is \fBtrue\fR.\&
.SS PIPELINE ROLES
.SS TRANSFORM
.PP
As a transform, the \fBtar\fR driver reads the directory tree\&
produced by the preceding driver and emits an uncompressed POSIX pax\&
archive stream.\&
Archives preserve filesystem metadata, ACLs, extended attributes, and\&
numeric ownership.\&
When the stream is stored by the \fBfile\fR driver, \fB.tar\fR\&
is appended to the artifact filename.\&
.SS zstd
.PP
The \fBzstd\fR driver uses the \fBzstd\fR utility to compress a\&
byte stream with Zstandard.\&
.SS CONFIGURATION
.PP
Specify \fBzstd\fR by name to use compression level \fB3\fR, or\&
give it an integer from \fB1\fR through \fB19\fR.\&
Higher levels generally improve compression at the cost of more time and\&
processing.\&
.SS PIPELINE ROLES
.SS TRANSFORM
.PP
As a transform, the \fBzstd\fR driver compresses the byte stream\&
produced by the preceding driver and emits a Zstandard-compressed\&
stream.\&
When it is stored by the \fBfile\fR driver, \fB.zst\fR is\&
appended to the artifact filename.\&
.SS gpg
.PP
The \fBgpg\fR driver uses the GnuPG \fBgpg\fR utility to encrypt\&
a byte stream with an OpenPGP public key.\&
.SS CONFIGURATION
.PP
The \fBgpg\fR value is the absolute path of the OpenPGP public-key\&
file used to encrypt the backup.\&
The path is interpreted on the system where the transform runs, and the\&
key does not need to be imported into a GnuPG keyring.\&
.SS PIPELINE ROLES
.SS TRANSFORM
.PP
As a transform, the \fBgpg\fR driver encrypts the byte stream\&
produced by the preceding driver and emits an OpenPGP-encrypted stream.\&
GnuPG\[aq]s internal compression is disabled; when compression is\&
wanted, a compression transform such as \fBzstd\fR should precede\&
\fBgpg\fR.\&
When the stream is stored by the \fBfile\fR driver, \fB.gpg\fR\&
is appended to the artifact filename.\&
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.PP
Global options must appear before the subcommand.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB-h\fB, \fB--help\fB\fR: Show top-level command-line\&
help and exit.\&
Every subcommand accepts the same option to show its own help.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB--version\fB\fR: Show the yaesm version and exit.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB-c FILE\fB, \fB--config FILE\fB\fR: Use\&
\fBFILE\fR as the configuration file for subcommands that read it.\&
The default is \fB/etc/yaesm/config.yaml\fR.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB--log-level LEVEL\fB\fR: Set the minimum logged severity\&
to \fBDEBUG\fR, \fBINFO\fR, \fBWARNING\fR, \fBERROR\fR,\&
or \fBCRITICAL\fR.\&
The default is \fBINFO\fR.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB--log-syslog[=ADDRESS]\fB\fR: Send logs to the syslog\&
socket at \fBADDRESS\fR.\&
When \fBADDRESS\fR is omitted, \fB/dev/log\fR is used.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB--log-stderr\fB\fR: Send logs to standard error.\&
Standard error is used automatically when no logging destination is\&
selected.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB--log-file FILE\fB\fR: Append logs to \fBFILE\fR.\&
.PP
Multiple logging destinations may be selected together.\&
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.PP
Where a subcommand accepts \fBTARGET[,TARGET...]\fR, each target may\&
name a backup or group.\&
Groups are expanded recursively, and each selected backup is operated on\&
once.\&
The special target \fB\[at]all\fR selects every configured backup\&
and cannot be combined with other targets.\&
.SS backup
.SS SYNOPSIS
.IP
.nf
\fR\&
yaesm [OPTIONS] backup [--schedule SCHEDULE] [--control-socket PATH] TARGET[,TARGET...]\&
\fR\&
.fi
.SS DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBbackup\fR subcommand asks a running yaesm scheduler to\&
execute the selected backups immediately.\&
It streams their log messages to standard error, waits for every\&
selected backup to finish, and exits unsuccessfully if any backup fails.\&
The scheduler must have been started with \fByaesm run\fR and must\&
use the same control socket.\&
.SS ARGUMENTS
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBTARGET[,TARGET...]\fB\fR: One or more backup or group\&
names, separated by commas.\&
.SS OPTIONS
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB--schedule SCHEDULE\fB\fR: Use the named on-demand\&
schedule for every selected backup.\&
If omitted, each backup must have exactly one on-demand schedule, which\&
yaesm selects automatically.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB--control-socket PATH\fB\fR: Connect to the scheduler\&
through this Unix socket.\&
The default is \fB/run/yaesm/control.sock\fR.\&
.SS check
.SS SYNOPSIS
.IP
.nf
\fR\&
yaesm [OPTIONS] check [--config-only] [-q] [TARGET[,TARGET...]]\&
\fR\&
.fi
.SS DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBcheck\fR subcommand verifies that the selected backups are\&
ready to run.\&
It should be used after creating or changing the configuration, before\&
relying on the configured backups, and as the first step when\&
investigating a backup problem.\&
Yaesm first validates the complete configuration, then performs\&
read-only checks to determine whether the selected backup pipelines can\&
run.\&
.PP
The checks depend on the drivers in each pipeline and may cover required\&
utilities, source and destination locations, filesystems, permissions,\&
encryption keys, and OpenSSH installation and connectivity.\&
Each check is reported as \fBPASS\fR or \fBFAIL\fR.\&
The command exits successfully only when the configuration is valid and\&
every performed check passes.\&
.SS ARGUMENTS
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBTARGET[,TARGET...]\fB\fR: Optional backup or group names,\&
separated by commas.\&
The default is \fB\[at]all\fR.\&
.SS OPTIONS
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB--config-only\fB\fR: Stop after validating the\&
configuration, without running driver checks.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB-q\fB, \fB--quiet\fB\fR: Show only failed checks.\&
.SS find
.SS SYNOPSIS
.IP
.nf
\fR\&
yaesm [OPTIONS] find TARGET[,TARGET...] [QUERY...] [--query QUERY...] [--schedule SCHEDULE[,SCHEDULE...]] [-0]\&
\fR\&
.fi
.SS DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBfind\fR subcommand lists existing backup artifacts for the\&
selected backups.\&
Each result is written to standard output as a destination-specific\&
locator.\&
Results are ordered newest to oldest within each backup.\&
Finding no matching artifacts is successful and produces no output.\&
.SS ARGUMENTS
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBTARGET[,TARGET...]\fB\fR: One or more backup or group\&
names, separated by commas.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBQUERY...\fB\fR: An optional query selecting artifacts by\&
creation time.\&
Omitting the query is equivalent to \fBall\fR.\&
.SS QUERIES
.PP
Queries are applied separately to each selected backup and use one of\&
the following forms:\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBall\fB\fR: Select every artifact.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBnewest\fB\fR: Select the newest artifact.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBoldest\fB\fR: Select the oldest artifact.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBafter TIME\fB\fR: Select artifacts created strictly after\&
\fBTIME\fR.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBbefore TIME\fB\fR: Select artifacts created strictly\&
before \fBTIME\fR.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBbetween TIME TIME\fB\fR: Select artifacts created at or\&
between the two times.\&
The times may be given in either order.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBclosest TIME\fB\fR: Select the artifact whose creation\&
time is closest to \fBTIME\fR.\&
If two artifacts are equally close, the newer one is selected.\&
.SS TIME VALUES
.PP
\fBTIME\fR may be a date in \fBYYYY-MM-DD\fR form, a date and\&
time in \fBYYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM\fR form, or a time on the current date\&
in \fBHH:MM\fR form.\&
A date without a time means midnight.\&
Date-and-time values may include \fBZ\fR or an ISO 8601 UTC offset;\&
values without an offset are interpreted in the configured scheduler\&
time zone.\&
Ambiguous local times require an explicit offset, and nonexistent local\&
times are rejected.\&
.PP
A relative time has the form \fBnow-Nm\fR, \fBnow-Nh\fR, or\&
\fBnow-Nd\fR, where \fBN\fR is a positive decimal integer\&
without leading zeros and the suffix selects elapsed minutes, hours, or\&
days.\&
.SS OPTIONS
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB-q QUERY...\fB, \fB--query QUERY...\fB\fR: Add\&
another query.\&
This option may be repeated; an artifact selected by any query is shown\&
once.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB-s SCHEDULE[,SCHEDULE...]\fB,\&
\fB--schedule SCHEDULE[,SCHEDULE...]\fB,\&
\fB--schedules SCHEDULE[,SCHEDULE...]\fB\fR: Limit results to\&
artifacts created by the named schedules.\&
Current or previous schedule names may be used, names may be\&
comma-separated, and the option may be repeated.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB-0\fB\fR: Terminate each result with a NUL byte instead\&
of a newline.\&
.SS run
.SS SYNOPSIS
.IP
.nf
\fR\&
yaesm [OPTIONS] run [--lockfile FILE] [--control-socket PATH] [--no-stderr-timestamps]\&
\fR\&
.fi
.SS DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBrun\fR subcommand starts the yaesm scheduler in the\&
foreground and runs configured timed backups until stopped.\&
It is generally launched and supervised by an init system.\&
It also creates the control socket used by \fByaesm backup\fR to\&
request immediate backups.\&
An exclusive lock prevents more than one scheduler using the same lock\&
file.\&
.SS SCHEDULING BEHAVIOR
.PP
Executions of the same backup are serialized.\&
An immediate execution, or an occurrence from another schedule for that\&
backup, waits for the current execution to finish.\&
If another timed occurrence of the same scheduled job becomes due while\&
its previous occurrence is still running, the new occurrence is skipped\&
and a warning is logged.\&
Different backups may run concurrently, subject to the configured\&
\fBmax_concurrent_backups\fR limit.\&
.SS OPTIONS
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB--lockfile FILE\fB\fR: Acquire the scheduler lock through\&
\fBFILE\fR.\&
The default is \fB/run/lock/yaesm-run.lock\fR.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB--control-socket PATH\fB\fR: Listen for control requests\&
on this Unix socket.\&
The default is \fB/run/yaesm/control.sock\fR; the socket is created\&
with mode \fB0600\fR.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB--no-stderr-timestamps\fB\fR: Omit timestamps from logs\&
written to standard error.\&
This is useful when an init system adds its own timestamps.\&
.SS SIGNALS
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBSIGHUP\fB\fR: Reload the configuration file selected by\&
\fB--config\fR.\&
A valid configuration becomes active for future scheduled and requested\&
backups without interrupting running backups.\&
If validation fails, the error is logged and the current configuration\&
remains active.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fBSIGINT\fB, \fBSIGTERM\fB\fR: The first signal begins\&
a graceful shutdown.\&
Yaesm stops accepting backups and configuration reloads, lets running\&
backups finish, then closes the control socket and exits.\&
A second signal forcibly terminates running backup commands and causes\&
yaesm to exit unsuccessfully; incomplete temporary or pending artifacts\&
may remain.\&
.PP
An init system should enforce a final stop timeout and forcibly\&
terminate yaesm and its remaining child processes if graceful shutdown\&
exceeds it.\&
.SH FILES
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB/etc/yaesm/config.yaml\fB\fR: Default configuration file.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB/run/lock/yaesm-run.lock\fB\fR: Default scheduler lock\&
file.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB/run/yaesm/control.sock\fB\fR: Default Unix socket used\&
for control requests between \fBbackup\fR and \fBrun\fR.\&
.SH EXIT STATUS
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB0\fB\fR: The requested operation completed successfully.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB1\fB\fR: The requested operation failed, a check or\&
backup failed, yaesm encountered an unexpected error, or scheduler\&
shutdown was forced.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB2\fB\fR: The command line was invalid.\&
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\fB78\fB\fR: The configuration could not be read or\&
validated.\&
